uality: Good. SEC-filed financials (Tier 1) for 12 years; guidance\nand RPO figures from secondary reporting of company calls (Tier 2/3);\nno segment data [Unconfirmed].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\nStep 1-1 Macro variables (T0 = Aug 2026):\n- 10Y Treasury: 4.71% (Aug 21, 2026) [Actual - FRED/TradingEconomics]\n- Fed funds target: 3.50\u20133.75%; FOMC on hold at July 2026 meeting\n  [Actual - J.P. Morgan / Charles Schwab research notes]\n- Fed stance: Cautious/Neutral; Powell signaling reluctance to cut with\n  inflation above target; market prices only ~1 additional 25bp cut in\n  2026 [Actual - Polymarket odds via search]\n- DXY: ~98.9\u201399.8, weak/drifting lower [Actual - TradingEconomics 2026-08-25]\n- S&P 500: 7,677; VIX: 15.45 [Actual - vendor quotes]\n- Inflation: Above 2% objective (Fed's July 2026 statement) [Actual]\n- Credit spreads: Not retrieved [Unconfirmed]\n- Real rates: 10Y TIPS implied real yield not retrieved; nominally 4.7%\n  10Y with ~2.5-3% core CPI \u2192 real ~1.8-2.2% [Estimate]\n\nStep 1-2 Regime probabilities [Assumption - my synthesis]:\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%\n- Reflation (high rates + growth): 35%\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 30%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 20%\nTotal = 100%.\n\nStep 1-3 Enterprise macro sensitivity (-3 to +3):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: +2 (long sales cycles; customer IT budgets\n  are rate-sensitive)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (FX was a $9M YoY headwind to RPO in Q2 2026;\n  weak dollar helps)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +2 (discretionary software deals)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: +2 (retail/wholesale capex correlated)\n- Commodity sensitivity: -1 (higher logistics costs can actually\n  accelerate automation demand \u2014 mildly positive)\n\nStep 1-4 Macro alignment:\nExpected macro impact: 0.15(+2) + 0.35(+1) + 0.30(-2) + 0.20(-1)\n= 0.30 + 0.35 - 0.60 - 0.20 = -0.15 \u2192 Neutral-to-mild Headwind.\nConviction: -0.5 steps. Split-entry strengthened if buying (we are not).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\nStep 1.5-1 Base rates:\n- High-ROIC enterprise software company (net cash, 25%+ op margin)\n  5-year survival: ~90% [Estimate - base rate]\n- Cloud transition success (installed base \u2192 SaaS) for established\n  software vendors completing within 5 years: ~65-75%\n  [Estimate - industry base rate]\n- WMS leader retaining #1/#2 Gartner position for 5 consecutive years:\n  ~60-70% [Estimate - based on 6 leaders in 2025 MQ]\n- Sustaining 10%+ revenue CAGR for 5 years starting from $1B+ revenue:\n  ~30-40% [Estimate - base rate for scaled software]\n\nStep 1.5-2 Deviation from base rate:\nMy base case assumes ~9% revenue growth for 5 years (2026-2030), which\nis BELOW the 10%+ threshold where base rates drop to 30-40%. Justification\nfor expecting above-base success: (1) RPO backlog $2.47B (+23% YoY) gives\n~2 years of revenue visibility [Actual - company call]; (2) cloud is only\n~43% of revenue with <25% of on-premise base converted \u2014 a multi-year\nmigration runway [Actual - company call]; (3) 18-time Gartner MQ leader\nwith partner-sourced deals up 4x [Actual/Estimate - company claims].\nDeviation from base rate