     129     139         \u2502\n\u2502 10.0%    \u2502  101     105     110     117     127         \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n[Estimate - sensitivity computed around base]\n\nStep 3-2 Multiples cross-check:\n- FY2026E GAAP-adjusted EPS: $5.47 [Actual - guidance midpoint, SBC\n  added back]. At $214.56: 39.2x forward adjusted P/E [Arithmetic]\n- FY2026E GAAP EPS (est. ~$4.30 after SBC + restructuring): ~50x\n  [Estimate]\n- FY2026E owner FCF/share: 253/58.6 = $4.32 [Arithmetic]. At $214.56:\n  49.7x owner FCF [Arithmetic]\n- Fair multiple for 8-10% grower with 25%+ owner FCF margin and wide\n  moat: 25-35x [Assumption - comparable quality compounders]. On\n  FY2027E owner FCF $4.88/share: fair value $122-171, midpoint $146.\n- EV/Sales FY2026E: 10.6x [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 3-3 Reverse DCF:\nTo justify $214.56, the company must grow revenue ~14% per year for a\ndecade (to ~$3.5-3.8B by 2035) at 32.5% terminal EBIT margin \u2014 with the\nSBC expense still taken out of margin. That is a 15%+ owner-FCF CAGR\nfor 10 years. The base rate for scaled software doing this is low\n(~30-40% for even 10% growth, lower for 14%). Reverse DCF verdict:\nEXCESSIVE relative to base rates.\n\nStep 3-4 Tornado \u2014 Top 3 value drivers:\n1. Revenue growth 2027-2030 (\u03c3 \u00b12pp changes IV by \u00b115%)\n2. WACC (\u03c3 \u00b11pp changes IV by \u00b112%)\n3. Terminal EBIT margin (\u03c3 \u00b12pp changes IV by \u00b18%)\nThese become Thesis KPIs in Section 8.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\nStep 3.5-1 Competitive response (-3 to +3): -2. SAP, Oracle, Blue Yonder,\nand Infor are all investing in cloud WMS (2025 Gartner MQ has 6 leaders\n[Estimate - Trace Consultants]). AI features will be copied within 18-24\nmonths. MANH's 18-time leader position is a lead, not a permanent gap.\n\nStep 3.5-2 Reflexivity: POSITIVE loop \u2014 high stock price funds SBC-based\ntalent retention, gives acquisition currency, and signals stability to\nenterprise buyers. NEGATIVE loop \u2014 if the stock corrects (as it did in\nJan 2025, -24.5%), services bookings historically wobble and attrition\nrisk rises. Currently the loop is constructive but fragile.\n\nStep 3.5-3 Customer behavior evolution: Early adopters (<25% of base)\nconverted. Mainstream adopters are slower, more price-sensitive \u2014 the\nEssentials mid-market tier addresses this [Actual - call]. As penetration\nrises, CAC rises and switching costs may compress for small clients.\nLTV/CAC and churn data ARE NOT DISCLOSED [Unconfirmed].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\nStep 4-1 Scenarios (probabilities sum to 100%):\n\u2500 BEAR (20%): Cloud decelerates to ~10% by 2028; services flat; license\n  shrinks faster; AI fails to monetize; services margin compression.\n  Revenue 2035: $1.43B; EBIT margin 25%; WACC 9.5%; TV g 2.5%.\n  IV \u2248 $70/share.\n\u2500 BASE (55%)